    "title": "datasette #3046: Don't serve link: rel=next HTTP headers to Firefox for HTML pages",
    "summary": "<p><strong><a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/datasette/issues/3046\">datasette #3046: Don&#x27;t serve link: rel=next HTTP headers to Firefox for HTML pages</a></strong></p> I was looking at the Firefox network panel today when I noticed that requests to a Datasette table page were triggering an <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Prefetch\">HTTP prefetch</a> to the next page of rows in that table.</p> <p>This was happening because Datasette pages serve an HTTP <code>link</",
    "body": "<p><strong><a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/datasette/issues/3046\">datasette #3046: Don&#x27;t serve link: rel=next HTTP headers to Firefox for HTML pages</a></strong></p> I was looking at the Firefox network panel today when I noticed that requests to a Datasette table page were triggering an <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Prefetch\">HTTP prefetch</a> to the next page of rows in that table.</p> <p>This was happening because Datasette pages serve an HTTP <code>link</code> header like this:</p> <p><code>link: <https://latest.datasette.io/fixtures/compound_three_primary_keys?_next=a%2Cd%2Cv>; rel=\"next\"</code></p> <p>This is bad. A tiny fraction of the visitors to one of those pages will click the \"next\" link, which means their browser is fetching unnecessary dat",
